# SOUNDNESS FIXTURE — the self-host lowerer must NOT resolve a WRITE PLACE
# through a `ReadGuard[T]`. A read view holds a shared lock; two readers
# holding concurrent guards must both observe the same value, so writing
# through one is UB. Rust gg's `try_resolve_field_place` closes it with a
# ReadGuard early-out (src/ir/lowering/exprs/mod.rs — the `is_read_only`
# arm), and the self-host now mirrors it at the WRITE-face caller:
# `amp_object_base` with `for_write=true` refuses to project through a
# ReadGuard base (`is_read_guard_type` — reads the typed
# `guard_method_prefix` channel, not a `ReadGuard__` substring). The READ
# arm (`lower_field_access`) is UNCHANGED: `guard_field_deref_base` still
# resolves all three guard variants uniformly for the read face.
#
# SAMPLED CELL (Core #12): a bare ReadGuard local as the projection base,
# with an `int` field. Track D's diff routed this through
# `guard_field_deref_base` unconditionally on the write face, so
# `inc(&rg.port)` mutated the guarded value and print observed `11`.
#
# GUARDED MECHANISM: the `for_write and is_read_guard_type(aob_name, gmod)`
# gate in `amp_object_base`. Delete the gate and the fixture reads `11` on
# the self-host again (measured).
#
# RED-VERIFIED against the pre-fix self-host (Track D commit 30d5f60d):
#   11
# Rust gg (oracle) and the post-fix self-host both print:
#   10
#
# `10` demonstrates the write was silently discarded — the value-read
# fallback path takes a value COPY of the field, `inc` mutates the copy, the
# copy dies at end-of-statement, and the guarded data is untouched. The
# ratified end-state is a check-time reject
# (`E_CannotWriteThroughReadView` — filed as a follow-up); pinning the
# `10` here traps regressions to the memory-unsafe `11` shape while the
# reject is designed.
from std.sync import RWLock

struct Config:
    int port

void inc(int &n):
    n = n + 1

void main():
    RWLock[Config] lock = RWLock[Config](Config(10))
    ReadGuard[Config] rg = lock.read()
    inc(&rg.port)
    print(rg.port)
